{"reason": "The execution results confirm severe overfitting of DeBERTa-v3-large (val loss spiking from 0.3057 to 0.95+ after epoch 4), and the optimal ensemble gives DeBERTa weight only 0.1, indicating embeddings do not generalize well. The initial plan proposes freeze-thaw fine-tuning, attention-pooled embeddings, stylistic augmentation, and focal loss. By refining these into module-level modifications with inspiration from successful historical experiments (which achieved stable training and better generalization through similar techniques), we focus on model_design (to implement freeze-thaw, attention pooling, and focal loss) and training_evaluation (to modify the training loop accordingly). Data processing remains unchanged as the handcrafted features and n-grams are already comprehensive.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Redesign the classification head: (1) Add a context-aware attention pooling layer that computes weighted average of all token embeddings from the last hidden state (instead of just [CLS]), using a learned linear layer with softmax to produce a 1024-d sentence vector. (2) Modify the loss function to use Focal Loss (gamma=2.0, alpha=balanced) instead of CrossEntropyLoss. (3) Increase dropout to 0.3 and weight decay to 0.1. Also, create two separate training modes: a 'frozen' mode where the encoder gradients are disabled, and a 'unfrozen' mode. The model's forward method should return both logits and the sentence embedding. Preserve the model class name and the methods (forward, etc.) as in the existing code (e.g., DebertaV2ForSequenceClassification wrapper).", "training_evaluation": "Modify the training loop: (1) Implement freeze-thaw: freeze encoder for first 3 epochs (set requires_grad=False for all encoder parameters), train only the head and attention pooling. (2) Use AdamW with weight decay 0.1, learning rate 1e-5 for unfrozen phase, and cosine annealing scheduler (T_max=10) instead of linear warmup. (3) Apply stylistic augmentation via a custom collate function in DataLoader: randomly substitute 20% of function words, insert archaic words with 10% chance, and perturb punctuation with 15% chance. (4) Track validation loss on original (unaugmented) data. (5) Keep early stopping (patience=5) and save best model. Interface constraints: keep variable names (train_loader, val_loader, model, optimizer, scheduler) unchanged; preserve the evaluate_deberta and compute_log_loss functions."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
